WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.040
GPU driver installation should start with a plan.

00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:06.645
Choose a Linux distribution and kernel strategy that fit the system's purpose.

00:00:06.645 --> 00:00:10.995
Long-term support distributions often favor stability and predictable updates.

00:00:10.995 --> 00:00:13.920
Rolling releases may provide newer kernels and libraries sooner,

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:15.670
but they can also introduce more change.

00:00:15.670 --> 00:00:19.640
AI systems that support real workflows usually benefit from controlled change.

00:00:19.640 --> 00:00:22.994
Repository packages and vendor packages each have tradeoffs.

00:00:22.994 --> 00:00:26.680
Distribution packages may integrate cleanly with the package manager and kernel update

00:00:26.680 --> 00:00:27.355
process.

00:00:27.355 --> 00:00:30.480
Vendor packages may provide newer support or specific versions.

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:34.610
Mixing sources without a plan can create dependency conflicts or unclear ownership of

00:00:34.610 --> 00:00:35.595
installed files.

00:00:35.595 --> 00:00:37.020
Pick an approach and document it.

00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:39.610
Version control for the stack is operational discipline.

00:00:39.610 --> 00:00:44.340
Record the distribution version, kernel, driver, firmware, CUDA or ROCm components,

00:00:44.340 --> 00:00:48.400
container runtime, inference runtime, Python environment, and relevant libraries.

00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:50.385
Pin versions when the system needs stability.

00:00:50.385 --> 00:00:55.285
Avoid unnecessary upgrades on stable AI systems unless there is a clear reason, test plan,

00:00:55.285 --> 00:00:56.280
and rollback path.

00:00:56.280 --> 00:00:58.045
Build a repeatable installation checklist.

00:00:58.045 --> 00:01:01.914
Include prerequisites, kernel headers, package sources, driver install method,

00:01:01.914 --> 00:01:05.600
Secure Boot considerations, runtime libraries, container configuration,

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:09.178
validation commands, smoke tests, and backup or snapshot steps.

00:01:09.178 --> 00:01:13.600
A checklist reduces guesswork and makes it possible to rebuild the same acceleration stack

00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:14.585
after a disk failure,

00:01:14.585 --> 00:01:16.100
migration, or failed upgrade.
